  ============================================================================
  THIS DOCUMENT GENERATES CODE. It is not a test fixture and not documentation.

  WHAT IT DRIVES
    src/UPnP.Rx/PortMapping/WanIpConnection.cs is a ~20-line partial class
    carrying [ScpdService("WANIPConnection1.scpd.xml")]. Every member it has -
    one typed method per <action> below, a nested record per action with
    out-arguments, and the argument range/allowed-value checks - is generated
    from THIS FILE at build time by ScpdServiceGenerator.

    Editing an <action> or <argument> name here renames a method or parameter
    for every consumer of the package. Generated members are public API with no
    deprecation path, so a change here is an API change: the public-API ledger
    (PublicAPI.Shipped.txt) and the checked-in snapshot
    (tests/UPnP.Rx.Analyzers.Tests/Snapshots/WanIpConnection.verified.cs) both
    fail the build until they are updated deliberately. That is the point, and
    it is verified - renaming one action here was tried, and both did fail.

  WHAT IT IS
    The standardized WANIPConnection:1 service template - a SUBSET of it. It
    describes what the SPECIFICATION declares, not what any particular router
    implements. A generated method compiling proves the template has that
    action; it proves nothing about the device in front of you, which may still
    answer with a SOAP fault. That asymmetry is why this library's parsers are
    lenient and why the generated wrapper never claims otherwise.

    Because it is a subset, it declares five of the seven actions
    InternetGateway uses - GetSpecificPortMappingEntry is missing, and
    AddAnyPortMapping is an IGD:2 action a ":1" document cannot contain. That is
    the sole reason InternetGateway still has a hand-written body rather than
    calling the generated wrapper. Completing this document is what unblocks it.

  HOW IT IS WIRED
    <AdditionalFiles Include="Scpd/WANIPConnection1.scpd.xml" /> in
    UPnP.Rx.csproj hands it to the compiler; the same file is packed at scpd/
    in the nupkg so consumers can read the exact document their wrapper came
    from. To generate a wrapper for your OWN service, do the same two things:
    check in an SCPD, add it as an AdditionalFiles item, and put
    [ScpdService("YourFile.scpd.xml")] on a partial class.

  Generated output (for reading, not editing) lands under
  src/UPnP.Rx/obj/<config>/net10.0/generated/.
  ============================================================================
-->

